Byte-correct the INT-8 ISR tick guard `c446==-1 && c2a8==0` (FUN_1000_31c3 + FUN_1000_3218).

The tick-bookkeeping gate in the ISR subtree is asm `inc byte [0x446]; jne skip; test byte [0x2a8],0xff;
jne skip` (base-0 0x13256) -- a per-tick reentrancy guard (DAT_1000_c446, the "in-tick" byte, resting
0xff; Ghidra folded the balanced inc/dec into the equivalent test `c446 == -1`) plus a byte test of
DAT_1000_c2a8. Ghidra typed BOTH as `undefined2` (word), so `DAT_1000_c446 == -1` compares the WORD at
0x1c446 (= c446|c447<<8 = 0x00ff, since c446=0xff and the adjacent c447=0) to 0xffff -> always false.
The whole `d8b6 += d8b8; if (carry && sema) c452++` body was therefore skipped every tick, so DGROUP:0x452
never advanced and FUN_1000_3346 hung -- even with the calibration (016), pump (017) and CS-based
semaphore (018) all correct. (Verified at runtime: the ISR ran 5965x and the outer c06b-block's
`d8be += d8c0` advanced, but d8b6 stayed 0 -> the c446 inner guard was the block.)

FIX: compare the low BYTE (asm-faithful): `(uint8_t)c446 == 0xff && (uint8_t)c2a8 == 0`. Class: byte
variable typed as word (docs/stage1.md; same family as patch 018's 2d58). The balanced inc/dec of c446
that Ghidra dropped are inert here (nothing in the guarded body reads c446).
